url: "https://laurelpoetry.com"
abstract: >-
  Laurel is a corpus of public domain English poetry in which every line has been scanned
  and most poems have been given a meter. It runs from Homer in English verse translation
  to the edge of the United States public domain in 1930. It grows with each release, so
  the counts of works, poems and lines are printed by the build script in the repository
  rather than repeated here.


  Every line is recorded twice. One pattern is the stress the words themselves settle,
  taken from a pronouncing dictionary and the poem's own syllabification. The other is the
  stress the meter asks for. Holding both is the point of the corpus. The difference
  between them is where a poet departs from the measure, and that is where most of the
  interest in meter lies. A corpus that stores one pattern and not the other cannot show
  it. Syllables the dictionary does not settle are marked as unsettled rather than guessed
  at. Stanzas carry a rhyme scheme lettered from rhyme keys, so that a sonnet's ABAB and a
  ballad's ABCB can be read by machine.


  The meter is not looked up. Every candidate is built as a template: the four feet at
  every length, with the licenses that real verse takes and the substitutions that English
  verse makes, such as the inverted first foot. Every syllable of the poem is then tested
  against each template. A poem whose verse will not settle is left without a meter rather
  than given a guess.


  The accuracy is measured rather than asserted, and it is reported in full. The scanner
  is scored against hand-annotated lines from three gold corpora, and again on the half of
  that data held out from every design decision. It is scored against works whose meter is
  documented in published scholarship. It is scored against forms such as the sonnet,
  where the form settles the meter and no annotation is needed. That last test catches
  what the others cannot: a scanner that has begun to offer confident wrong answers to
  poems that were never in doubt. The current figures and the failures are in the
  documentation.


  Every poem has a canonical reference on the CTS model, and the reference names its
  edition. A citation to a poem is not reproducible unless it says which text it means.
  Every poem also has a permanent identifier that survives retitling and re-parsing. Many
  poems carry line-level timings against public domain recordings, so that a reading can
  be followed line by line.


  The corpus is the material underneath laurelpoetry.com, a free reading room that I
  maintain. The same data is set out there for reading and teaching: rhyme lettered on the
  page, stress marked against the poem's own meter, hard words glossed from a period
  dictionary, editors' commentary, a short course in prosody, printable worksheets, and
  exercises in scanning and naming forms. Nothing there costs anything and nothing is
  behind a login.


  The texts are in the public domain. The encoding, the scansion and the annotation are CC
  BY-SA 4.0. The method is documented in METHOD.md, including the places where it is weak.
  Free verse is still given a meter it does not have. Editions were chosen for being in
  the public domain rather than for being the best witness.


